      "text": "Given that 20$\\%$ of 10$\\%$ of a number is 12, we can set up the equation as follows:\n\n\\[\n0.20 \\times 0.10 \\times x = 12\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n0.02x = 12\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{12}{0.02}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 600\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find 10$\\%$ of 20$\\%$ of the same number. This can be represented as:\n\n\\[\n0.10 \\times 0.20 \\times x\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = 600 \\):\n\n\\[\n0.10 \\times 0.20 \\times 600\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 0.02 \\times 600\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 12\n\\]\n\nThus, 10$\\%$ of 20$\\%$ of the number is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
